  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x201c;VIHas de Vida&#x201d;, holistic response to HIV and AIDS in Jalisco, Mexico</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2007 it is estimated that 11,000 people died of AIDS in Mexico. Mexico HIV prevalence (the proportion of the population with HIV) is between 150,000 and 310,000. Mexico represents nearly 10% of the total of HIV cases in the continent and, according to UNAIDS, AIDS is the fourth cause of death amongst male Mexicans between the ages of 25-34 years. In Jalisco, the areas with higher prevalence are Guadalajara City, Zapopan, Puerto Vallarta, Ciudad Guzm&#xe1;n, Tlaquepaque and Tonal&#xe1;. Estimates are 42 thousand people living with the virus and not knowing.
92% of infections in Mexico are through sexual transmission. Nearly 60% of the HIV diagnoses to date, in Mexico can be attributed to unprotected sex between men. 
Mexico provides antiretroviral (ARV) drugs to 57% people with HIV &amp; AIDS-related illnesses. 
831,584 people aged 15 years and over received HIV testing and counseling from Jan to Dec 2006 in Mexico and know the result.
Two of the Mexican states (Nayarit and Colima) have criminalized willful HIV transmission. 
This project aims to develop a more holistic response to HIV that both reduces vulnerability and social stigma to infection and improves the quality of life for 62 men and 43 women living with HIV and 720 people affected by HIV (family members) in 3 municipalities of Jalisco (Puerto Vallarta, Ciudad Guzm&#xe1;n and Tonal&#xe1;), Mexico, through human and spiritual development processes, education, information and human rights defence.</narrative>
  </description>
